  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q6",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "In this question we will prove the following theorem. When two circles intersect, the line joining their centres bisects that common chord at right angles.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove $\\triangle ACD\\cong\\triangle BCD$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [
          {
            "id": "p17-q6-diagram",
            "role": "question",
            "format": "tikz",
            "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source page 17 p17-q6-diagram\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[line width=0.8pt,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\pgfmathsetmacro{\\chordx}{(1.9*1.9-1.65*1.65+2.4*2.4)/(2*2.4)}\n\\pgfmathsetmacro{\\chordy}{sqrt(1.9*1.9-\\chordx*\\chordx)}\n\\coordinate (C) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (D) at (2.4,0);\n\\coordinate (E) at (\\chordx,0);\n\\coordinate (B) at (\\chordx,\\chordy);\n\\coordinate (A) at (\\chordx,-\\chordy);\n\\draw (C) circle[radius=1.9];\n\\draw (D) circle[radius=1.65];\n\\draw (C)--(B)--(D)--(A)--cycle;\n\\draw (C)--(D) (A)--(B);\n\\fill (C) circle[radius=1.5pt];\n\\fill (D) circle[radius=1.5pt];\n\\node[left=2mm] at (C) {$C$};\n\\node[right=2mm] at (D) {$D$};\n\\node[above] at (B) {$B$};\n\\node[below] at (A) {$A$};\n\\node[below left=1mm] at (E) {$E$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
            "src": null,
            "widthMm": 72,
            "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
            "overlayOf": null,
            "transparent": false,
            "axes": null,
            "spec": {
              "sourcePage": 17,
              "sourceRegion": "Question 6 part a shared figure",
              "description": "Two intersecting circles, with the left circle larger. Centres C and D lie on CD. Intersections B above and A below are joined by the common chord AB, meeting CD at E. All four radii CA, CB, DA and DB are drawn. The figure is shared by parts a–c.",
              "mathematics": "CA=CB and DA=DB are radii of their respective circles. E is the intersection of AB and CD. Construction coordinates determine the circle intersections; no numerical measurements are printed.",
              "answerVisibility": "No perpendicularity mark, bisection ticks or congruence annotations are supplied."
            },
            "derived": false,
            "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
          }
        ],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\triangle ACD\\cong\\triangle BCD$ (SSS).",
          "worked": "$AD=BD$ (radii of the circle with centre $D$).\n\n$AC=BC$ (radii of the circle with centre $C$).\n\n$CD$ is common.\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle ACD\\cong\\triangle BCD$ (SSS).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove $\\triangle ACE\\cong\\triangle BCE$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\triangle ACE\\cong\\triangle BCE$ (SAS).",
          "worked": "$AC=BC$ (radii).\n\n$\\angle ACE=\\angle BCE$ (corresponding angles in the congruent triangles from part a, since $C,E,D$ are collinear).\n\n$CE$ is common.\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle ACE\\cong\\triangle BCE$ (SAS).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove $CD\\perp AB$ and $BE=EA$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$CD\\perp AB$ and $BE=EA$.",
          "worked": "$\\angle BEC=\\angle CEA$ (corresponding angles in the congruent triangles from part b).\n\nThese angles lie on the straight line $AB$, so each is $90^\\circ$. Therefore, $CD\\perp AB$.\n\nAlso, $BE=EA$ (corresponding sides in $\\triangle ACE\\cong\\triangle BCE$).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      }
    ]
  },
